00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:12.360
Let's get into
this a little bit.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.850
From a history
perspective, we had

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:17.250
two databases that sat on prem.

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:18.870
We had one analytics database.

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:21.120
We had one real-time database.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:24.590
For the real-time database,
we had SOAP-based interactions

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:28.350
that become pretty inflexible
as you're moving forward,

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:31.890
and we were just continually
building on tech debt,

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:35.610
essentially, so every team
had their own operations.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:37.890
We were stuck with 90
operations that pretty much do

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:39.880
the same thing over
and over again,

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:42.480
which becomes a challenge,
but these really

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:44.340
did just get your
customer information,

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.740
update your customer
information, get your points.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.230
Pretty simplistic
stuff that we knew

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:51.040
we couldn't use moving forward.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:53.190
The other piece to this
is just that operations

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:54.338
is kind of a nightmare.

00:06:54.338 --> 00:06:56.130
If we want to integrate
with third parties,

00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:58.588
that's going to be a couple
months, because we have to do--

00:06:58.588 --> 00:07:01.380
from a security perspective,
we're just mostly going off

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:03.150
of firewall rules,
things like that,

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:05.580
that take two to three
months with our existing

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:08.173
infrastructure, so not what
we wanted to build upon.

00:07:08.173 --> 00:07:09.840
The second piece of
this is that we just

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:16.180
had XML files going
down every week that

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:19.180
changed to about every day, to
do points, give rewards, give

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:19.680
tiers.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.080
We've kind of lost the customer
engagement at that point.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:25.097
Also, a lot of operations,
just a lot of pinprick things,

00:07:25.097 --> 00:07:27.180
that we have to deal with
that we just didn't want

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.070
to deal with moving forward.

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:31.582
The last piece of this is we
had an analytics database.

00:07:31.582 --> 00:07:33.290
People would just run
huge queries trying

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:35.730
to do models that would
tear it down, break it

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:37.147
in the middle of
the night, people

00:07:37.147 --> 00:07:40.560
have to wake up just to
challenge-- we have to scale.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.230
We had to move forward to that,
so we're starting to do that.

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:45.237
We had three options here.

00:07:45.237 --> 00:07:47.070
One option is we could
just keep building it

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:49.590
on prem with the
things we wanted to do.

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:52.110
That clearly wasn't
what we could do.

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:55.350
We could buy it off the shelf,
but then at some point--

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:58.270
other customers are using this,
other companies are using this,

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:01.002
so we're not really
different than them.

00:08:01.002 --> 00:08:02.460
So we decided to
move to the cloud.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:04.272
We decided to
partner with Slalom.

00:08:04.272 --> 00:08:05.730
They were huge in
helping us really

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:07.620
change our mindset
in architecture

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.738
and helping us build
this moving forward.

00:08:10.738 --> 00:08:12.780
The challenge ahead of us
was that we didn't have

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:16.030
a lot of cloud experience--

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:17.160
very in our infancy.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:18.910
We had a couple of
applications out there,

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:21.030
but nothing huge like
loyalty was going to be.

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:22.650
But also, like
Rich talked about,

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:25.490
there was no central person
or team that owned this.

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:27.180
It was all tribal knowledge.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:28.972
I'd be going meeting
to meeting finding out

00:08:28.972 --> 00:08:30.700
something different,
but at some point,

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:32.533
we landed on a couple
of things, and we just

00:08:32.533 --> 00:08:34.470
had to move forward and go.

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:37.000
We did that, but then we
got to our requirements,

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:39.203
like the worst requirement
you can ever deal with,

00:08:39.203 --> 00:08:41.370
which is all current features
and functionality must

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:43.148
be implemented in
the new system.

00:08:43.148 --> 00:08:44.940
That's just kind of
like you open up a box,

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:46.398
and everything's
shaken up, and you

00:08:46.398 --> 00:08:48.580
have no idea what's going on.

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:50.250
But we were pretty eager.

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:52.350
I'm a millennial, so I
just jump in and think

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:53.433
I'm going to live forever.

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:58.620
We just jumped into
it, got excited,

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:00.203
but we wanted to
build a community,

00:09:00.203 --> 00:09:01.870
not necessarily just
a loyalty platform.

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:03.340
We wanted to build a community.

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:04.710
This is what we
wanted to build it on.

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:05.790
We wanted to be API first.

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:07.248
We wanted to enable
anything that's

00:09:07.248 --> 00:09:10.050
in loyalty to be outside
facing that we can interact

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:12.660
with our customers, not
just our regular customers,

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:14.460
but also third parties,
anyone who wants

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:15.886
to connect to our platform.

00:09:15.886 --> 00:09:18.120
The second piece to this
is being developer-centric,

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:19.203
so no manual deploys.

00:09:19.203 --> 00:09:21.370
You don't have to wake up
in the middle of the night

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:22.350
to deal with things.

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:24.480
We're going to reheal
ourselves, and also, we're

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:26.522
just going to enable a
lot of automation and even

00:09:26.522 --> 00:09:28.488
third-party integrations.

00:09:28.488 --> 00:09:30.780
The biggest piece of moving
to the cloud, like everyone

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:33.195
will talk about, just the
flexibility, scalability,

00:09:33.195 --> 00:09:35.370
and stability, and
that's the biggest thing

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:38.850
was just application
analytics for us.

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:41.400
We can't have our
real-time databases

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:44.092
or real-time applications
getting impacted by models.

00:09:44.092 --> 00:09:46.050
So we've got to break
that apart and figure out

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:47.580
how we're going to scale.

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:49.782
Then the last thing,
just real-time analytics.

00:09:49.782 --> 00:09:51.240
The big thing that
we dealt with is

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:55.050
that we wanted to stream data
all the time in the old system,

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:58.080
but we have an on-premise server
that has a bunch of licenses.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:09:59.760
We don't want to
buy more licenses,

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:03.028
so we're only going to take
the data that we can take,

00:10:03.028 --> 00:10:05.070
and that's going to hinder
our analytics as we're

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:07.278
moving forward, because
we're not getting everything.

00:10:07.278 --> 00:10:10.280
We're just getting pieces of it.

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:11.960
How should we build loyalty?

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:13.430
That's the biggest piece.

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:16.320
We need to build a platform
first and keep moving forward.

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:18.360
We need to modernize
that as well.

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:21.130
So what we did is we jumped in
Kubernetes just like everyone

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:22.250
is talking about.

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:24.540
We used Spring
Boot Java services,

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:27.420
whether that was synchronous
or asynchronous communication,

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:29.177
so API or event driven.

00:10:29.177 --> 00:10:31.010
We're going to do that
to essentially enable

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:33.552
our platform so it can scale to
the traffic and the customers

00:10:33.552 --> 00:10:34.880
that we're going to have.

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:37.925
We ended up creating seven
to nine microservices.

00:10:37.925 --> 00:10:39.800
If you want to talk
about technical details--

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:41.508
I'm going to stay
pretty high level here,

00:10:41.508 --> 00:10:44.537
so we can talk after
or hit up the Dory--

00:10:44.537 --> 00:10:47.120
but we needed to make sure that
we could scale moving forward.

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:49.443
We have a pretty small team,
especially starting off,

00:10:49.443 --> 00:10:51.860
and so dealing with a bunch
of operations, that's just not

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:53.810
going to work for us.

00:10:53.810 --> 00:10:55.880
So seven to 10 microservices.

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:59.240
A couple that really
handle most of our APIs,

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:01.410
just because of how large
it's working right now,

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:03.160
but then the rest were
really event-driven

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:05.690
and could scale up,
scale down pretty easily.

00:11:05.690 --> 00:11:10.710
The biggest thing you will
hear every retailer talk about

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:13.677
is you have a bunch of servers
for two months of the year,

00:11:13.677 --> 00:11:16.010
because that's when all the
customers are coming to you.

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:18.572
That's when everyone's
coming to you saying, hey,

00:11:18.572 --> 00:11:19.530
we need this right now.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:20.410
We need to shop.

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:22.040
We need to buy
everything last minute.

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:23.995
That's kind of what
happens, but you've

00:11:23.995 --> 00:11:26.120
got servers sitting there
for the rest of the year,

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:27.110
and they have licenses.

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:29.695
You've got to pay for all that,
keep the lights on for it,

00:11:29.695 --> 00:11:31.820
so we want to make sure
we're using what we can use

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:33.800
and using it when we
need it, when customers

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:36.050
are coming to us, when third
parties are coming to us,

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:38.830
things like that.

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:40.120
We got our loyalty platform.

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:41.610
It could scale.

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:44.740
It can interact pretty easily,
but how do we secure our APIs?

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:45.990
Everyone talks about security.

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:47.407
You need to have
it, but we needed

00:11:47.407 --> 00:11:48.960
to enable that pretty easily.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:51.950
We moved to Apigee, and
I've used Apigee previously,

00:11:51.950 --> 00:11:54.180
so this was a pretty
simple decision.

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:55.560
Using this as our
gateway enabled

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:58.923
a lot of the easy
security features for us.

00:11:58.923 --> 00:12:00.840
Also enabled a lot of
third-party integration,

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:03.660
so being able to spin up a
developer portal pretty easily,

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:05.690
putting YAMLs out
there, allowing people

00:12:05.690 --> 00:12:08.430
to just see it, interact
with it, ask for an API key,

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:09.840
ask for auth.

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:13.330
Easy to interact with, good
for us as we're moving forward

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:15.780
and we're building this
developer community.

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:17.280
And the biggest
piece that we really

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:20.490
didn't know about that really
helped us moving forward

00:12:20.490 --> 00:12:21.853
was the payload transformation.

00:12:21.853 --> 00:12:23.520
I'll talk about this
a little later when

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:26.050
it talks about
implementation, but we

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:28.150
have a decent amount
of legacy systems,

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:31.700
and we couldn't change
those legacy systems,

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:35.276
so that was a huge pain
as we got into things.

00:12:35.276 --> 00:12:37.810
But we got around
it, made things work,

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:39.130
and Apigee was huge in that.

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:43.760
How are we going to
enable analytics?

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:45.230
Honestly, really
simple decision.

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:47.110
I'll show you our
pattern after this.

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:49.100
Moved to BigQuery.

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:50.480
We can easily scale everything.

00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:52.340
There's hardly any operations.

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:54.020
Data storage is cheap.

00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:57.320
We're able to query petabytes
of data in 30 seconds--

00:12:57.320 --> 00:13:00.337
if we optimize it,
maybe 15 seconds--

00:13:00.337 --> 00:13:02.420
as we're moving forward
and create whatever models

00:13:02.420 --> 00:13:03.860
we want to for our
analytics team.

00:13:06.850 --> 00:13:08.200
It's a pretty easy decision.

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:09.552
I blank out on it because it
was just the easy decision

00:13:09.552 --> 00:13:10.094
that we made.

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:15.067
How do we get information
into the system and out

00:13:15.067 --> 00:13:15.650
of the system?

00:13:15.650 --> 00:13:16.660
This was the last piece.

00:13:16.660 --> 00:13:19.420
We've got our APIs
and API enablement.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:21.010
We've got our loyalty platform.

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:23.293
We have our data warehouse.

00:13:23.293 --> 00:13:25.460
So cool, we've got everything,
but how do we stream?

00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:26.620
How do we get data in?

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:28.780
This is pretty common for us.

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:30.940
Again, Slalom's huge with this.

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:31.960
Pretty simple process.

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:35.830
Drop a file into a
bucket, Cloud Functions,

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:37.930
and kick off a
Dataflow job, and then

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:39.800
spin up a serverless
architecture,

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:43.660
boom, right to Cloud
SQL, right to BigQuery.

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:46.283
Pretty simple decisions.

00:13:46.283 --> 00:13:48.950
We continue to do this as we are
also interacting with our APIs,

00:13:48.950 --> 00:13:53.260
so streaming-based
jobs enable us

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:55.960
to really-- anything we add
to our Cloud SQL database

00:13:55.960 --> 00:13:58.450
or any new API we
add, we can just

00:13:58.450 --> 00:14:00.340
update our objects comment.

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:02.583
It'll pick it up as
we redeploy the job,

00:14:02.583 --> 00:14:04.000
and boom, we can
stream everything

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:04.990
to any of our tables.

00:14:07.602 --> 00:14:09.560
The last piece was how
do we enable developers.

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:11.420
We could have
easily built loyalty

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:13.280
and just said, here's
loyalty, but we're

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:15.650
going to hurt ourselves
moving forward.

00:14:15.650 --> 00:14:17.930
We need to enable developers,
because there were so

00:14:17.930 --> 00:14:20.292
many times that we were
waiting on resources

00:14:20.292 --> 00:14:21.250
for two to three weeks.

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:22.190
And it's not
anyone's fault, it's

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:25.115
just you've got to get servers,
or you've got to get licenses,

00:14:25.115 --> 00:14:26.392
or you've got to get whatever.

00:14:26.392 --> 00:14:28.100
The biggest thing with
going to the cloud

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:30.560
is that you can
get rid of all that

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:32.893
and not have to
deal with any of it.

00:14:32.893 --> 00:14:35.060
The biggest piece we used
is infrastructure as code,

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:36.500
which would be Terraform.

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:38.870
Terraform allowed us to
just do pull requests

00:14:38.870 --> 00:14:41.870
and have uniform environments,
uniform naming standards,

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:43.490
everyone can see
everything, but we

00:14:43.490 --> 00:14:45.740
can build out whatever we
need at the time at which we

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:46.250
need it.

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:49.375
As we're moving forward,
we can get new Pub/Subs,

00:14:49.375 --> 00:14:50.750
we can get a new
database, we can

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:52.566
get whatever we
need in a fraction

00:14:52.566 --> 00:14:54.800
of an hour versus a week.

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:56.040
Great for us.

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:57.560
The last piece was
just the CI/CD.

00:14:57.560 --> 00:14:59.340
We're still working
on this a little bit,

00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:02.007
but this was a pretty simple one
of just using Bitbucket storing

00:15:02.007 --> 00:15:04.820
all our code, and every time
something goes to Bitbucket,

00:15:04.820 --> 00:15:07.910
Jenkins kicks off,
pushes a build out there.

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:08.630
We're good to go.

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:09.980
Readiness checks
blue-green, so we

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:11.570
don't have to deal with anything
in the middle of the day,

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:13.190
and we can deploy
at any point in time

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:16.340
and not have to worry about
doing this overnight and pretty

00:15:16.340 --> 00:15:20.380
simple, pretty easy
stuff to do with Jenkins.

00:15:20.380 --> 00:15:22.330
This is where it gets tricky.

00:15:22.330 --> 00:15:24.627
We had everything that
we wanted to have.

00:15:24.627 --> 00:15:25.960
We were really excited about it.

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:27.910
And then a couple
months down the road,

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:30.280
we've reloaded the
database about 30 times,

00:15:30.280 --> 00:15:32.603
changed the schema
about 60 times,

00:15:32.603 --> 00:15:34.270
and we're all pretty
frustrated and just

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:37.000
have to deal with how
implementation goes.

00:15:40.112 --> 00:15:42.523
Learning the new cloud
platform, there's

00:15:42.523 --> 00:15:43.690
a little bit of a challenge.

00:15:43.690 --> 00:15:45.315
There's a little bit
of mindset change,

00:15:45.315 --> 00:15:48.000
but the biggest thing is moving
all your existing challenges

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:51.338
and problems and getting rid of
those and moving to the cloud.

00:15:51.338 --> 00:15:53.380
You can easily, if you
want to spin up a machine,

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:56.200
put your stuff on a
machine, that's pretty easy.

00:15:56.200 --> 00:15:57.340
We didn't want to do that.

00:15:57.340 --> 00:15:59.750
We wanted to set ourselves
up for the future

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:03.000
and keep moving there, so
we get an implementation.

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:05.880
We're going into every
meeting, and that requirement

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:09.450
of everything existing
has to stay the same

00:16:09.450 --> 00:16:11.100
just kills us every single time.

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:13.020
We're coming in,
have a new feature

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:17.130
about every other meeting, and
we're probably six months down

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:19.083
the road at this point.

00:16:19.083 --> 00:16:21.000
We're dealing with all
these end requirements,

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:24.080
so whether it's tech debt
with logic built in APIs,

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:27.990
logic built in stored procedures
that those APIs are calling,

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:30.180
just the network latency.

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:32.460
And it's not the
cloud's latency.

00:16:32.460 --> 00:16:34.860
It was our latency
that we didn't

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:38.097
want to admit to at times,
but that was a big challenge.

00:16:38.097 --> 00:16:40.680
And the other challenge is that
we just weren't really set up.

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:42.570
You're never going to be ready
when you're moving straight

00:16:42.570 --> 00:16:43.590
to the cloud.

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:45.000
You've just kind
of got to do it.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:46.500
You're going to
have the challenges.

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:48.630
There's going to be pain
points, but just do it.

00:16:48.630 --> 00:16:51.002
It's going to be worth
it in the future,

00:16:51.002 --> 00:16:52.710
but you're going to
have to deal with it.

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:55.300
The last one is integrating
with legacy applications,

00:16:55.300 --> 00:16:57.678
whether it was
[INAUDIBLE] XML, people

00:16:57.678 --> 00:16:59.220
are looking for
these certain fields.

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:01.510
There's a bunch of
transformations in the APIs

00:17:01.510 --> 00:17:03.980
that aren't real
in the database.

00:17:03.980 --> 00:17:05.099
We dealt with it all.

00:17:05.099 --> 00:17:07.641
We dealt with those problems,
and really, the biggest problem

00:17:07.641 --> 00:17:08.790
was just day-to-day data.

00:17:08.790 --> 00:17:11.473
We didn't necessarily
know what our data was,

00:17:11.473 --> 00:17:13.140
and that's just because
of the tech debt

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:16.160
we'd built up over years.

00:17:16.160 --> 00:17:17.390
We get through it all.

00:17:17.390 --> 00:17:19.390
We're starting to
get some steam,

00:17:19.390 --> 00:17:20.910
and we get about
three weeks out.

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:22.410
And we're like,
man, we just totally

00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:23.900
forgot about this piece.

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:26.359
We didn't have any
dashboards set up.

00:17:26.359 --> 00:17:30.140
We weren't sure what we needed
from an alert standpoint,

00:17:30.140 --> 00:17:31.640
but luckily--

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:33.320
this is my one pitch to Google--

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:36.352
Stackdriver was awesome.

00:17:36.352 --> 00:17:38.060
It easily integrates
with everything else

00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:40.970
you're going to do,
whether it's latency,

00:17:40.970 --> 00:17:43.500
APIs with Google
endpoints, alerts,

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:45.670
dashboards-- you
name it, it's got it.

00:17:45.670 --> 00:17:47.960
It was awesome for us,
really easy to set up,

00:17:47.960 --> 00:17:49.940
and we could just have
everything overnight,

00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:51.490
and we still use it today.

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:52.610
Pretty awesome.

00:17:52.610 --> 00:17:55.860
Also, just with error reporting,
huge for us for bug visibility.

00:17:55.860 --> 00:17:57.620
We could figure out
who made this call

00:17:57.620 --> 00:18:00.292
at this time, what operation
died at that point,

00:18:00.292 --> 00:18:01.250
and what was the issue.

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:08.010
We get to the last
three weeks, and I

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:11.310
know I did some 7:00
AM to 2:00 AM shifts,

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:15.140
so it was kind of
tough at that point.

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:17.720
But luckily, everything
actually went pretty flawlessly.

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:18.620
We had a bunch--

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:20.390
a couple, not a
bunch-- but we had

00:18:20.390 --> 00:18:23.720
a couple of scares going
into the night we go live.

00:18:23.720 --> 00:18:27.320
Probably the best story
is the one day we actually

00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:29.840
were about to go live,
and we had to call it off

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:31.580
because our points
were messed up.

00:18:31.580 --> 00:18:35.716
And then Kevin and team brought
us a cake that day for go live.

00:18:35.716 --> 00:18:38.190
That felt great.

00:18:38.190 --> 00:18:40.280
But that was one of
the funny stories.

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:42.690
I think a couple days later,
we actually did go live.

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:43.875
RICHARD CLUM: Yeah.

00:18:43.875 --> 00:18:46.370
CAM HEIGHTLAND: You can look
back and laugh at it now.

00:18:46.370 --> 00:18:47.420
But yeah, it was tough.

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:50.402
It was tough during
the point in time.

00:18:50.402 --> 00:18:51.360
But yeah, we went live.

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:54.560
And honestly, we were so
close to Black Friday,

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:57.500
it was a little bit
of a concern for sure.

00:18:57.500 --> 00:18:59.090
But honestly, we
had a lot of peace

00:18:59.090 --> 00:19:01.810
of mind moving to the cloud.

00:19:01.810 --> 00:19:03.687
With everything we
had had, we were

00:19:03.687 --> 00:19:05.270
killing all of our
performance numbers

00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:08.160
without actually
optimizing everything.

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:10.340
So we hit a ton of
numbers that we wouldn't

00:19:10.340 --> 00:19:13.760
have hit in previous programs.

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:16.520
Things that would go
down during peak times

00:19:16.520 --> 00:19:17.720
were easily scaling up.

00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:20.578
And we had no problems at all.

00:19:20.578 --> 00:19:22.370
Black Friday time, we
were hitting numbers.

00:19:22.370 --> 00:19:24.762
We hit numbers that we've
never hit before, which

00:19:24.762 --> 00:19:25.970
were huge for us and awesome.

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:31.280
But what I'll say is that
with going to the cloud,

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:34.100
Google's been great
partners, awesome partners,

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:35.600
but moving to the
cloud itself, it's

00:19:35.600 --> 00:19:37.160
just peace of mind
at some point,

00:19:37.160 --> 00:19:39.063
for us, with a team
that's pretty small

00:19:39.063 --> 00:19:40.730
and have to do deal
with the operations,

00:19:40.730 --> 00:19:42.220
have to do with everything.

00:19:42.220 --> 00:19:44.450
I had come from
on-prem world where

00:19:44.450 --> 00:19:46.560
I'd be up in the
middle of the night.

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:48.393
I don't have to worry
anymore at this point.

00:19:48.393 --> 00:19:49.893
I know things are
going to scale up.

00:19:49.893 --> 00:19:52.062
I know things are going
to reheal themselves.

00:19:52.062 --> 00:19:54.520
And everything's a lot easier
as we've been moving forward.

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:56.513
Especially even
building new features,

00:19:56.513 --> 00:19:58.430
everything's a lot easier
because I don't have

00:19:58.430 --> 00:19:59.760
to worry about my resources.

00:19:59.760 --> 00:20:02.570
I don't have to worry about
what I need to develop the tools

00:20:02.570 --> 00:20:05.600
and move our program forward.

00:20:05.600 --> 00:20:06.550
So here's what we did.

00:20:06.550 --> 00:20:09.900
This is what we started with.

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:11.505
We weren't 100% sure.

00:20:11.505 --> 00:20:13.380
We're like, this is
really a lot of the tools

00:20:13.380 --> 00:20:14.530
that we thought we needed.

00:20:14.530 --> 00:20:16.630
But we didn't need these
tools to move forward,

00:20:16.630 --> 00:20:18.720
and this is what we ended with.

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:21.480
The biggest piece of
this is we're continuing

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:22.840
to add new features every day.

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:25.350
[INAUDIBLE] Data
Fusion, Cloud Run,

00:20:25.350 --> 00:20:28.010
which looks awesome for us.

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:29.730
But things are
getting a lot easier.

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:31.522
And we're able to get
these really quickly.

00:20:31.522 --> 00:20:34.852
If we would have went with a
third party, essentially just

00:20:34.852 --> 00:20:37.435
someone we bought off the shelf,
or if we would have bought it

00:20:37.435 --> 00:20:40.050
on prem, there's no way we would
have made it at a year's time

00:20:40.050 --> 00:20:41.903
to actually deploy this thing.

00:20:41.903 --> 00:20:43.320
There were so many
changes that we

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:46.030
had to do not because
of bad architecture,

00:20:46.030 --> 00:20:47.820
but it was what we
knew at the time.

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:50.250
We were able to change
easily throughout time

00:20:50.250 --> 00:20:54.460
and continually change as we're
moving forward on the platform.

00:20:54.460 --> 00:20:56.410
So I'm going to give
this to Rich now.

00:20:56.410 --> 00:20:58.702
If you have any questions,
feel free to catch me after.

00:20:58.702 --> 00:21:00.510
Feel free to ask the Dory.

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:02.500
And thank you.

00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:03.740
RICHARD CLUM: Thanks, Cam.

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:06.370
So yeah, I want to talk you
through where we're at today.

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:07.900
Obviously, enabling
this platform

00:21:07.900 --> 00:21:09.317
for all of our
marketing partners,

00:21:09.317 --> 00:21:13.180
our analytics partners to
really drive this forward.

00:21:13.180 --> 00:21:14.680
So there's some
quick stats on where

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:15.930
we're at since we've launched.

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:18.520
Again, this was September
25th, a day that will forever

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:21.160
be stuck in my mind.

00:21:21.160 --> 00:21:22.750
Some quick numbers we've done.

00:21:22.750 --> 00:21:24.730
One that we're very
proud of is that top one

00:21:24.730 --> 00:21:27.310
up there in the corner, rated
number one most innovative

00:21:27.310 --> 00:21:30.190
loyalty program by Shopify.

00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:32.772
Something obviously we pat
ourselves on the back about.

00:21:32.772 --> 00:21:34.480
But realized now that
we've launched this

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:36.772
and we're out there, we
have to keep that going.

00:21:36.772 --> 00:21:38.230
We've reached the
top very quickly.

00:21:38.230 --> 00:21:39.790
We appreciate that.

00:21:39.790 --> 00:21:41.290
A lot of things went into that.

00:21:41.290 --> 00:21:45.100
And now, obviously,
we want to stay there.

00:21:45.100 --> 00:21:46.000
Cam mentioned this.

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:48.040
We went close to holiday.

00:21:48.040 --> 00:21:50.498
So think about it, that's two
months prior to Black Friday.

00:21:50.498 --> 00:21:52.873
This past Black Friday, we
hit some of the highest sales,

00:21:52.873 --> 00:21:54.760
the highest sales numbers
on an hourly basis

00:21:54.760 --> 00:21:56.530
that DSW has ever seen.

00:21:56.530 --> 00:21:58.450
And that number
speaks for itself.

00:21:58.450 --> 00:22:00.328
Absolutely zero issues
on our platform.

00:22:00.328 --> 00:22:02.620
We were going through holiday
readiness plans and like,

00:22:02.620 --> 00:22:03.460
who's going to be on site?

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:04.180
Who's going to do this?

00:22:04.180 --> 00:22:06.513
And we're like, we're going
to go ahead and support this

00:22:06.513 --> 00:22:07.035
from remote.

00:22:07.035 --> 00:22:07.660
We'll sleep in.

00:22:07.660 --> 00:22:08.870
If you call us, need us.

00:22:08.870 --> 00:22:11.070
Zero phone calls.

00:22:11.070 --> 00:22:12.950
The other thing is we
moved into the cloud.

00:22:12.950 --> 00:22:14.533
And again, we went
from an environment

00:22:14.533 --> 00:22:16.250
that didn't move very quickly.

00:22:16.250 --> 00:22:19.180
We've done, since go live, over
900 enhancements and bug fixes

00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:20.800
to our platform.

00:22:20.800 --> 00:22:23.860
That kind of goes with a big
pat on the back for our friends

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:26.320
at Slalom, helping us move
to that agile platform,

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:27.903
as well as what the
cloud has given us

00:22:27.903 --> 00:22:30.400
from a deliverability standpoint
to continuously improve

00:22:30.400 --> 00:22:33.820
and move upon what's out there.

00:22:33.820 --> 00:22:35.770
Another big one
was, again, trying

00:22:35.770 --> 00:22:38.140
to stay in contact
with our customers

00:22:38.140 --> 00:22:40.420
to do mobile pushes from
the app notifications.

00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:42.257
Based on previous
system limitations,

00:22:42.257 --> 00:22:44.590
we had to break our audience
into eight different groups

00:22:44.590 --> 00:22:45.680
and slowly send it out.

00:22:45.680 --> 00:22:46.460
Make sure it sent.

00:22:46.460 --> 00:22:48.400
Make sure we didn't
kill the network.

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:51.187
Through performance testing
as we led up to the holidays,

00:22:51.187 --> 00:22:53.020
we got that down to one
big group of people.

00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:55.062
And it's like, all right,
we're going to send it.

00:22:55.062 --> 00:22:57.520
And we brought up Stackdriver,
monitoring the logs.

00:22:57.520 --> 00:23:00.100
OK, there was the
blip, and we're done.

00:23:00.100 --> 00:23:02.710
And it wasn't even, like you had
to zoom in and find the blip.

00:23:02.710 --> 00:23:03.430
Like did it really hit?

00:23:03.430 --> 00:23:04.370
Did you guys actually do this?

00:23:04.370 --> 00:23:05.170
We fine?

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:07.752
100% fine.

00:23:07.752 --> 00:23:09.960
Ongoing opportunities, so
obviously, we're out there.

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:12.570
We're live, moving through it.

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:15.220
As Cam mentioned, I mentioned,
we have a very small team.

00:23:15.220 --> 00:23:18.525
So it's the continuous
growth of our engineers.

00:23:21.930 --> 00:23:24.120
We have a couple on
our team individually.

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:25.620
We have a strong
contractor base.

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:28.010
And again, partnered with
Slalom has been ongoing.

00:23:28.010 --> 00:23:29.760
But we just need to
continue growing them.

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:30.450
We're new to the cloud.

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:31.020
We understand it.

00:23:31.020 --> 00:23:32.640
But this platform and our
partnership with Google

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:34.680
has allowed us to continuously
grow and understand

00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:37.180
that the sky's the limit from
where we want to go internally

00:23:37.180 --> 00:23:38.610
as a team.

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:40.920
Cam hit on this as
well, our DevOps.

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:43.500
So we had DevOps as
a DSW organization

00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:46.902
for the on-prem
platforms that we had.

00:23:46.902 --> 00:23:48.360
But now we're
talking cloud DevOps.

00:23:48.360 --> 00:23:49.995
That means something
different to them.

00:23:49.995 --> 00:23:51.870
When they're asking,
OK, how are your backups

00:23:51.870 --> 00:23:52.920
going to be handled?

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:54.120
When are your patching
going to handle?

00:23:54.120 --> 00:23:55.050
What are you scheduled for that?

00:23:55.050 --> 00:23:56.190
We're like, I don't know.

00:23:56.190 --> 00:23:56.940
Let's call Google.

00:23:56.940 --> 00:23:57.832
Let's find out.

00:23:57.832 --> 00:23:59.290
We didn't have to
worry about that.

00:23:59.290 --> 00:24:01.165
We had our guys focused
on what was important

00:24:01.165 --> 00:24:03.900
and that was delivering those
900-plus fixes ongoing day

00:24:03.900 --> 00:24:04.837
after day.

00:24:04.837 --> 00:24:06.420
By the way, if you
do the math, that's

00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:08.518
like five per day,
business days,

00:24:08.518 --> 00:24:09.810
that we've deployed since then.

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:11.940
So pretty staggering numbers.

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:13.440
But it's getting
that DevOps side up

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:15.440
to speed because we know
we're going to continue

00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:16.847
to develop on and on and on.

00:24:16.847 --> 00:24:18.930
And we need to make sure
that back end is handled,

00:24:18.930 --> 00:24:20.310
incidents are
properly addressed,

00:24:20.310 --> 00:24:21.870
data things are there,
so I can keep the people

00:24:21.870 --> 00:24:23.790
like Cam developing the
front end things that

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:25.177
are important to us.

00:24:25.177 --> 00:24:27.010
And alongside of that
is just the education.

00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:29.610
So obviously, we've been
living and breathing

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:31.800
Google Cloud for
over a year now,

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:36.120
working very close with Kevin
and team, getting out there.

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:40.930
But groups like our audit team,
our security team, our database

00:24:40.930 --> 00:24:42.723
admins, how can we perform this?

00:24:42.723 --> 00:24:43.890
And what can we do for them?

00:24:43.890 --> 00:24:45.730
Again, we don't
need you to do that.

00:24:45.730 --> 00:24:47.100
But here's how you help us.

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:49.740
Here's how you can
help us move forward

00:24:49.740 --> 00:24:52.650
to keep DSW moving in
the right direction.

00:24:52.650 --> 00:24:53.590
Team challenges.

00:24:53.590 --> 00:24:54.810
So we're very proud.

00:24:54.810 --> 00:24:56.760
We've built this
j time application

00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:59.760
that performs at scale that does
everything we need it to do.

00:24:59.760 --> 00:25:01.720
But we still live in
a batch-based world.

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:03.810
So every night, we still have
to take snapshots, get it down

00:25:03.810 --> 00:25:04.812
to our on-prem database.

00:25:04.812 --> 00:25:07.020
We still have to get that
data down to third parties.

00:25:07.020 --> 00:25:09.960
We're still working through
those legacy systems and legacy

00:25:09.960 --> 00:25:12.067
applications to make
sure they're whole.

00:25:12.067 --> 00:25:13.650
But it makes it
difficult. And they're

00:25:13.650 --> 00:25:15.960
like, OK, we sent the data
down to EDW last night.

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:18.000
Can you run a query
to validate that?

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:18.505
Yes, I can.

00:25:18.505 --> 00:25:19.380
I can go to BigQuery.

00:25:19.380 --> 00:25:20.618
I can use all my data dates.

00:25:20.618 --> 00:25:22.160
But our application
is built to know,

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:27.450
what are you doing in the moment
for that real-time snapshot?

00:25:27.450 --> 00:25:28.665
Project tech debt.

00:25:28.665 --> 00:25:31.500
So timeline compromises,
things led to,

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:33.960
we just can't get this done
in the timeline we need to.

00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:35.460
There were some analytics
things they needed

00:25:35.460 --> 00:25:36.793
or some data things they needed.

00:25:36.793 --> 00:25:38.220
We're like, we'll get to that.

00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:40.410
But we had a deadline
we needed to meet.

00:25:40.410 --> 00:25:42.480
But via the agile
process using JIRA,

00:25:42.480 --> 00:25:44.070
using the process
we have, we know

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:45.140
we can just recover from that.

00:25:45.140 --> 00:25:47.557
And we're not going to build
ourself a 12-year period tech

00:25:47.557 --> 00:25:50.612
debt that we absorbed as
we took on this project.

00:25:50.612 --> 00:25:52.320
And that kind of leads
into the last one.

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:53.487
So our backlog is out there.

00:25:53.487 --> 00:25:55.278
We know what it is we
have to work through.

00:25:55.278 --> 00:25:57.780
But now we've enabled a process
for our marketing partners

00:25:57.780 --> 00:26:00.540
to realize that they can
get stuff as quickly as they

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:01.800
can almost ask for it.

00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:03.972
So we went live in September.

00:26:03.972 --> 00:26:05.430
Just here in the
next couple weeks,

00:26:05.430 --> 00:26:07.110
we have another pretty
big release coming out.

00:26:07.110 --> 00:26:09.568
We already have another one
scheduled for the fall as well.

00:26:09.568 --> 00:26:11.670
So as I'm looking through
that trying to make sure

00:26:11.670 --> 00:26:13.420
that we are making
sure that we prioritize

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:17.010
to dig through our backlog,
make sure our tech debt doesn't

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:18.960
get up there, as well
as we're making sure

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:20.460
that we can support
the business for

00:26:20.460 --> 00:26:21.880
their ongoing opportunities.

00:26:24.335 --> 00:26:25.960
As I mentioned to
start with, we really

00:26:25.960 --> 00:26:27.910
didn't have a marketing
team or an IT team

00:26:27.910 --> 00:26:29.780
to support our
marketing partners.

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:32.650
So as this came along,
again, my team was stood up,

00:26:32.650 --> 00:26:34.840
and I brought on people
like Cam and other folks

00:26:34.840 --> 00:26:38.200
to give our DSW marketing
partners not just one team

00:26:38.200 --> 00:26:40.938
to deal with, the Designer
Brands Marketing IT team,

00:26:40.938 --> 00:26:43.480
but obviously, we have a great
partnership with Google Cloud.

00:26:43.480 --> 00:26:44.897
And our partners
at Slalom as well

00:26:44.897 --> 00:26:48.670
help us really build from what
was a very disjointed process

00:26:48.670 --> 00:26:51.550
to a collaborative group that
continue moving us forward.

00:26:51.550 --> 00:26:54.688
Slalom came in, again, day
one of our project, really

00:26:54.688 --> 00:26:56.230
helped us build out
that architecture

00:26:56.230 --> 00:26:58.910
that Cam walked you through.

00:26:58.910 --> 00:27:01.447
But it wasn't a go
live's done, we're out.

00:27:01.447 --> 00:27:02.280
They're still there.

00:27:02.280 --> 00:27:04.113
They're still helping
us build our strategy,

00:27:04.113 --> 00:27:07.210
making sure that as we're
looking for what we have done

00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:09.010
and what we're doing
forward, we're not

00:27:09.010 --> 00:27:10.588
putting ourself into a hole.

00:27:10.588 --> 00:27:12.130
And they've been a
great partnership.

00:27:12.130 --> 00:27:14.868
It's been, even though
they're based out of Atlanta,

00:27:14.868 --> 00:27:16.660
we're based out of
Columbus, we didn't even

00:27:16.660 --> 00:27:17.650
see that as a problem.

00:27:17.650 --> 00:27:19.480
The interaction and
communication, we never

00:27:19.480 --> 00:27:20.170
caught them off guard.

00:27:20.170 --> 00:27:21.628
And they never
caught us off guard.

00:27:24.890 --> 00:27:27.180
And then growth, so as Kevin
mentioned to start with,

00:27:27.180 --> 00:27:29.180
when we launched this and
started going into it,

00:27:29.180 --> 00:27:32.470
we were DSW Inc.
Since then, we just

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:33.700
recently rebranded ourself.

00:27:33.700 --> 00:27:36.370
And as a parent company,
we're Designer Brands Inc.

00:27:36.370 --> 00:27:41.380
What that really means is we
as a IT shared services support

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:42.400
all these brands now.

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:46.060
So DSW VIP is live for the US.

00:27:46.060 --> 00:27:47.270
We have DSW Canada.

00:27:47.270 --> 00:27:50.230
So we're in the process of
moving them over to the cloud

00:27:50.230 --> 00:27:51.730
and using our same
loyalty platform

00:27:51.730 --> 00:27:53.568
for our Canadian-based stores.

00:27:53.568 --> 00:27:56.110
Shoe Co. and Shoe Warehouse have
a loyalty program also based

00:27:56.110 --> 00:27:57.902
in Canada that we're
in the process, again,

00:27:57.902 --> 00:27:59.810
rewriting that, moving
it to the cloud.

00:27:59.810 --> 00:28:02.770
They quickly saw the benefits
that we had and implemented

00:28:02.770 --> 00:28:05.995
for our US-based stores and
asked not, can we have it?

00:28:05.995 --> 00:28:07.263
It's, can I get it tomorrow?

00:28:07.263 --> 00:28:08.680
They understand
what kind of value

00:28:08.680 --> 00:28:10.685
we're driving to keep
their program moving.

00:28:10.685 --> 00:28:13.060
We also have the Camuto Group
and our affiliated business

00:28:13.060 --> 00:28:16.368
groups that typically they come
in, OK, hey, can you do this?

00:28:16.368 --> 00:28:17.410
Can you guys handle this?

00:28:17.410 --> 00:28:19.540
Cam and I are sitting
there going, essentially,

00:28:19.540 --> 00:28:20.235
bring it on.

00:28:20.235 --> 00:28:21.610
Give me more people
to help do it

00:28:21.610 --> 00:28:22.870
because we need it to happen.

00:28:22.870 --> 00:28:24.995
But the cloud has given us
a platform that we don't

00:28:24.995 --> 00:28:26.590
have to say no to anymore.

00:28:26.590 --> 00:28:28.150
Analytics team, we
can enable them.

00:28:28.150 --> 00:28:29.000
The data is there.

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:30.970
They're not asking us
to remodel anything.

00:28:30.970 --> 00:28:31.780
Here's BigQuery.

00:28:31.780 --> 00:28:32.467
You go after it.

00:28:32.467 --> 00:28:34.300
If you need more stuff,
we'll get it to you.

00:28:34.300 --> 00:28:35.592
But we can get it to you quick.

00:28:37.870 --> 00:28:39.370
And Cam kind of hit
on this as well,

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:40.912
and this is kind of
our thing that we

00:28:40.912 --> 00:28:43.500
have realized as we've gone
through the whole process is

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:45.160
there was no shortcuts
of getting to the cloud.

00:28:45.160 --> 00:28:46.785
We wanted to make
sure we did it right.

00:28:46.785 --> 00:28:49.320
But since we've gone live,
we've recognized fully

00:28:49.320 --> 00:28:52.490
that it's 100% worth it.

00:28:52.490 --> 00:28:53.353
No looking back.

00:28:53.353 --> 00:28:55.020
No questions, did we
do the right thing?

00:28:55.020 --> 00:28:57.090
Did we not do the right thing?

00:28:57.090 --> 00:28:59.385
It's been since
literally the first time

00:28:59.385 --> 00:29:00.802
we started streaming
data into it,

00:29:00.802 --> 00:29:02.385
and we're like, OK,
that data was bad.

00:29:02.385 --> 00:29:03.270
We have to redo it.

00:29:03.270 --> 00:29:04.228
It was quick to fix it.

00:29:04.228 --> 00:29:07.500
Quick to move forward
and just recognize

00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:10.320
the benefits of the data
stores, the opportunities,

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:12.800
the data flows that we've used.

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:15.240
Our friends at Slalom can
vest for how many times we

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:17.450
asked them to pivot and change.

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:19.200
But the cloud gave
us that capabilities.

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:20.310
The infrastructure scales.

00:29:20.310 --> 00:29:22.660
Everything there helped us out.

00:29:22.660 --> 00:29:25.780
And again, that growth slide
that we just talked to,

00:29:25.780 --> 00:29:27.280
limits of where
we're going and what

00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:29.470
we're going to do for
Designer Brands as a whole.

00:29:29.470 --> 00:29:33.720
